From 5783a7ce61ff2b4986907dda794aa4c516f2ad60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aubrey Pule Khoza <aubreypk@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:15:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Split the interface into a student view and a lecturer
 view

The platform has two audiences that want opposite things. A learner wants
somewhere to work; a lecturer wants to see what the model decided and why.
Showing both every view at once is most of what made the interface read as an
admin console: the design console and the model reference sat in a student's
face, and the ontology was on screen while they were trying to answer a
question.

Student sees activities only. Lecturer gets the same list as any chosen
learner, a new curriculum view listing every criterion with its classification
and flag, the design console, and the model reference. The criteria table on an
activity opens by default for a lecturer and stays folded for a student, and
the link into the console is a lecturer's.

It is a view switch, NOT authentication -- there are no accounts and no
participants, and the learner selector stands in for a login. Said plainly in
the README and in a comment above the routes, so Chapter 5 states it rather
than implying access control the prototype does not have.

Also fixes the learner dropdown, which was reported as dead. The markup and the
handler were correct; the styling was not. The popup list is drawn by the
browser rather than by the stylesheet, so setting a colour on the select alone
left the options inheriting near-white text on whatever background the platform
chose -- light, on Windows. The list opened invisible, which reads exactly like
a broken control. Styling option and optgroup is the fix.

The curriculum view uses /api/curriculum, which existed and had no caller.

Checked: tags and delimiters balanced across all six components, every one of
the 57 class tokens the JSX names is defined in the stylesheet, the criterion
field names match what the API returns, and the 33 API checks still pass.

